 ## Submission and Retrieval
 
 Authenticate the submitting worker and validate field types and plot references before accepting a submission. Preserve the original payload separately from any later resolved or combined representation. A retry with the same key and identical payload returns the original acceptance result; reuse with a different payload is rejected.
 
-Retrieval must distinguish a usable current state from unresolved review work. Exactly how those states are represented remains part of the open choice.
+Retrieval distinguishes a usable current state from unresolved review work. A visit's current state is either a single resolved revision (when no competing edits exist) or marked as having unresolved review work (when competing revisions exist).
+
+## Data Model
+
+### Visit
+
+| Field | Type | Description |
+|-------|------|-------------|
+| visit_id | UUID | Client-generated, immutable |
+| plot_id | UUID | References catalog plot |
+| revision | integer | Monotonically increasing, starts at 1 |
+| observation_date | date (ISO 8601) | |
+| observers | string[] | List of observer names |
+| species_cover | object[] | Each: { species_code, cover_percent } |
+| notes | string | Free text |
+| base_revision | integer | The revision this edit was based on (for submissions) |
+
+### Submission
+
+| Field | Type | Description |
+|-------|------|-------------|
+| submission_id | UUID | Server-generated |
+| visit_id | UUID | |
+| revision | integer | The revision number this submission proposes |
+| base_revision | integer | The revision the worker started from |
+| payload | object | Complete visit fields (observation_date, observers, species_cover, notes) |
+| author_id | string | Worker identifier |
+| submitted_at | timestamp | Server receipt time |
+| idempotency_key | string | Client-generated, unique per submission attempt |
+
+### Resolution
+
+| Field | Type | Description |
+|-------|------|-------------|
+| resolution_id | UUID | Server-generated |
+| visit_id | UUID | |
+| resolved_revision | integer | The new current revision |
+| chosen_submission_id | UUID | The submission chosen as the basis (or a new merge) |
+| coordinator_id | string | Coordinator who resolved |
+| resolved_at | timestamp | |
+| contributing_submission_ids | UUID[] | All submissions considered in this resolution |
+
+## Submission Envelope
+
+A submission request carries:
+- `idempotency_key` (string, required)
+- `visit_id` (UUID, required for edits; absent for new visits)
+- `base_revision` (integer, required for edits; absent for new visits)
+- `payload` (object, required): complete visit fields
+  - `plot_id` (UUID)
+  - `observation_date` (date)
+  - `observers` (string[])
+  - `species_cover` (object[])
+  - `notes` (string)
+
+The server validates:
+- `plot_id` exists in the catalog
+- `observation_date` is a valid date
+- `cover_percent` values are integers 0–100
+- `species_code` values are recognized (catalog-managed or free-text per policy)
+
+On success, returns:
+- `submission_id`
+- `visit_id` (new or existing)
+- `revision` (the assigned revision number)
+- `status`: `accepted` | `conflict` (when base_revision ≠ current revision)
+
+## Current-State Response
+
+A visit retrieval returns:
+- `visit_id`
+- `current_revision` (integer)
+- `current_payload` (object): the resolved visit fields
+- `status`: `resolved` | `unresolved`
+- `unresolved_submissions` (array, present when status = `unresolved`): each with `submission_id`, `revision`, `base_revision`, `payload`, `author_id`, `submitted_at`
+
+When `status = resolved`, `current_payload` is the authoritative visit. When `status = unresolved`, `current_payload` is the last resolved revision (or null for a brand-new visit with competing first submissions), and `unresolved_submissions` lists the competing revisions awaiting review.
+
+## Unresolved-Work Representation
+
+A visit is `unresolved` when two or more submissions share the same `base_revision` and that `base_revision` equals the visit's current revision at the time the first of those submissions arrived. Subsequent submissions that also base on that same revision join the unresolved set. Submissions that base on an older revision are rejected with `status: conflict` and must be rebased by the tablet.
+
+An unresolved visit has no single current payload — it has a set of competing complete revisions. The coordinator chooses one submission's payload as the basis for the new revision (or constructs a new payload), creating a new resolved revision.
+
+## Resolution Operation
+
+A coordinator resolves an unresolved visit by:
+- Selecting one competing submission as the basis, OR
+- Providing a new complete payload (manual merge)
+
+The system creates a new revision with:
+- `resolved_revision = current_revision + 1`
+- `chosen_submission_id` (the selected submission, or null for manual merge)
+- `contributing_submission_ids` = all submissions in the unresolved set
+- `coordinator_id`, `resolved_at`
+
+All submissions in the unresolved set are marked as resolved by this resolution. The visit status becomes `resolved` with the new payload.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Concurrency policy | Whole-visit revisions | Field-aware three-way merge | User preference: accepts larger review queue over maintaining merge rules that could combine scientifically related edits without noticing. Field-aware merge would automatically combine disjoint field changes (e.g., observer name correction + species addition) and only flag overlapping changes for review, reducing review volume. However, it requires maintained merge rules for each field type (scalar fields, observer lists, species arrays), and the rules cannot capture scientific relationships — e.g., changing a species code and its cover estimate together is a related edit that the rules would treat as independent, potentially producing a combined record that misrepresents the observation. The team prefers explicit coordinator review of every competing revision over implicit combinations that may silently cross scientific boundaries. |
+| Revision numbering | Monotonic integer per visit | Timestamp-based, UUID per revision | Simple ordering, easy for workers to communicate, fits offline gap |
+| Submission payload | Complete visit (all fields) | Partial/delta fields | Whole-visit policy requires complete revisions; simplifies validation and review comparison |
+| Conflict detection | Base revision mismatch | Last-write-wins, vector clocks | Preserves both submissions; aligns with "do not silently discard" tenet |
+| Idempotency | Client key + payload match | Server-only deduplication | Works offline; survives retries across disconnections |
+
+## Downstream Contract Implications
+
+This section defines what tablet-sync and review-export must accommodate given the whole-visit revisions policy.
+
+### Tablet-Sync (submission & local state)
+
+**Local storage must retain:**
+- For each in-progress visit: the `base_revision` the worker started from (the current revision at fetch time)
+- For each submitted visit: the `submission_id`, `revision` proposed, and `base_revision` used
+- The complete catalog snapshot used for plot references
+
+**Submission flow:**
+- On first submission of a new visit: send payload without `visit_id` or `base_revision`
+- On edit of existing visit: send `visit_id`, `base_revision` (the revision the worker's draft was based on), and complete payload
+- On `status: conflict` (base_revision ≠ current server revision): the tablet must rebase — fetch the latest visit state, present the worker with the competing revisions, and let them produce a new edit from the current revision
+- On `status: accepted`: store the returned `submission_id` and `revision`; the visit is now either `resolved` (no competing edits) or `unresolved` (competing edits exist)
+
+**Conflict presentation to worker:**
+- When a conflict occurs, the tablet receives the current visit state with `status: unresolved` and the `unresolved_submissions` array
+- The worker must be shown all competing complete revisions (their payloads, authors, submission times) alongside their own draft
+- The worker produces a new complete visit payload based on the current revision (which becomes the new `base_revision`)
+
+**Offline considerations:**
+- Workers can draft visits offline using the cached catalog and the last known `base_revision`
+- Submissions queue locally and send when connectivity returns
+- Idempotency keys survive app restarts and multi-day disconnection
+- A worker may have multiple pending submissions for different visits simultaneously
+
+### Review-Export (coordinator interface & CSV production)
+
+**Review queue:**
+- All visits with `status: unresolved` appear in the review queue
+- For each unresolved visit, the coordinator sees: the last resolved revision (if any), and all competing submissions with their complete payloads, authors, and submission times
+- The coordinator resolves by either:
+  - Selecting one submission's payload as the new current revision, OR
+  - Constructing a new complete payload (manual merge) and recording which submissions were consulted
+- Resolution creates a new revision with `contributing_submission_ids` listing all submissions in the unresolved set
+
+**Export eligibility:**
+- Only visits with `status: resolved` are eligible for the Friday CSV export
+- Unresolved visits are held out of the export until resolved
+- The export includes the current resolved payload for each eligible visit, with no revision history in the CSV (the partner receives only the current state)
+
+**Provenance & audit:**
+- The review-export interface must show the full submission history for any visit (all submissions, resolutions, coordinators, timestamps)
+- This enables coordinators to answer "how did this value get here?" questions from the science partner
+- The `contributing_submission_ids` on each resolution provides the trace from exported value back to original worker submissions
 
